I don't seem to be able to figure out how to make "rsh" work for uid 0
under AXP OSF/1.  Suppose host "foo" is an AXP running OSF/1, "spam" is
some other UNIX system (doesn't matter what flavour), and "spam root" is
in the /.rhosts file on "foo".  If I'm logged in as root on "spam", and
type
	rsh foo date

I get the following error message:

	rshd, : No such file or directory

Just putting "hostname root" in /.rhosts works fine under other UNIXes,
and I'm ~99% certain that it worked in the pre-release beta test version
of DEC OSF/1 (T1.2-2 Rev 5), but I can't check for certain because the
system with that version isn't here any longer.

I've tried obvious things such as putting other variants of the hostname
into the /.rhosts file (e.g. the fqdn), but the symptom remains the same.
By the way, "rsh" works fine for uids other than 0, by putting appropriate
information into /etc/hosts.equiv (or the user's $HOME/.rhosts).

I've read the man pages for rsh, rshd, and other related things, but I've
not found anything that might suggest where the problem lies.  (The above
error message, by the way, isn't even mentioned in the rshd man page.)

I'm stumped; any suggestions?

