Part One:	How to compile freely available software.
 
################################################################################

This is a list of freely available software running on "my" Alpha and
compilation hints I collected from the net. 

Good news first: some well written pieces of software compile without
	any problems on the Alpha. Among them are: 
	
	- bison		GNU Bison version 1.20
	- gzip	 	gzip 1.0.6
	- less		less  version 177E and 178
	- patch 	Patch version 2.0, patch level 12g8
	- syd		- S Y D  -  System Display Program  Version 3.1
		  	found on ftp.rrz.Uni-Koeln.DE
			- nearly as nice as 'top' 
			- install it setuid to root.
	- unzip 	UnZip:  Zipfile Extract v5.0 of 21 August 1992
	- xdvi	 	xdvi for X11, patchlevel 16 (-DBMSHORT in the DEFS)

Most software however need more or less porting effort.

# bash		- needs patch posted to comp.unix.osf.osf1 on 2 Apr 93 15:28
		  by arnej@lise.unit.no (Arne H. Juul)
		- there is a Version on ftp.ncsa.uiuc.edu in /DEC_Alpha

# dvips 	- dvips-5.515
		- compile with cc (works fine for most documents when compiled
		  with gcc but fails on test.tex from distribution)
		- remove '-Wall -funsigned-char' from OPT line in Makefile
		- afm2tfm needs gcc, however

# gcc		- gcc-2.3.3 needs Johan Vromans' (jv@mh.nl) patch found
		  in comp.unix.osf.osf1;
		- There is a version called gcc-2.3.3+-alpha on 
		  crl.dec.com:/pub/DEC 
		- fails "make compare" but generates fine executables
		- -g is not supported
		- GNU C++ and GNU Objective-C are included, but do not work

# ghostscript   - gs-252 compiles smoothly; 
		- produces lots of "unaligned access" warnings for text files;
		  warnings can be swiched off by "uac p 0" at the shell level 
		  (or "uac s 0" on startup); see Ultrix manpage for "uac".
		- PS files generated from dvips(5.47 or 5.515) on Alpha or any 
		  other machine are not viewed successfuly: some show up with
		  pages left blank, some produce segmentation faults
		- A bug report of thimm@ira.uka.de was answered by a promise 
		  for a new release of ghostscript.
		- kivinen@cs.hut.fi (Tero Kivinen) posted a patch to  
		  gnu.ghostscript.bug and comp.sys.dec on 23 Apr 1993, claiming
		  to cure the unaligned access problem on BL12 machines.
		  (It doesn't on my BL10...)

# gnu-tar       - vprintf seems to be broken on the Alpha (BL10) ; 
  gnu-find	  must remove #define HAVE_VPRINTF=1
		  in Makefile after running ./Configure

# perl		- needs Mike Iglesias' <iglesias@draco.acs.uci.edu> patch 
		  found in alpha-osf-managers mailing list on 08 Mar 93;
		  (archived on ftp.kpc.com, see Part 3).
		- use 'dec-osf' instead of 'osf1', which is the default
		  running ./configure;

# tcsh-6.03	- use cc (gcc fails with #include problems) 
		  and config/config.osf1
		- in Makefile: watch for "DEC osf1 on the alpha"
		  	       CFLAGS= $(INCLUDES) -O -Olimit 2000 

# TeX-3.141	- change lib/config.h: #define INTEGER_TYPE int (was: long)
		  (thanks to bruhn@wu-wien.ac.at)
		- use gcc-2.3.3; (cc is said to work, too)
		- switch off all optimisation, at least with 'make MF';
		- virmf needs additional library: -ldnet_stub:
		  change ./Makefile:  wlibs= -lXt -lX11 -ldnet_stub
		- (passed Trip and Trap tests on 5 Apr 93)

# tiff library  - see xv

# Monsanto NQS  - Kalle@rrz.uni-koeln.de (Claus Kalle) put patch to
		  ftp.Uni-Koeln.DE:/decosf/src/nqs-3.31.1.patch1.decosf

# Wcl + Mri (Widget Creation Library +  Motif Resource Interpreter) 
		- Wayne Scott (wws@isp.bellcore.com) compiled them
		  without major problems

# xgobi (a statistics program, interactive dynamic graphics program for
		data visualization)
		- Joern Amundsen <joern@imf.unit.no> made a patch set for the
		  mar-93 version of xgobi

# xgopher	- arnej@imf.unit.no (Arne Henrik Juul) writes:
		  xgopher compiled fine on T1.2-2 when I just put -DSYSV
		  in the Makefile produced by xmkmf. 

# xv 		- ftp.ncsa.uiuc.edu has xv-3.0 and xv-2.21.NEW in /DEC_Alpha
		  (note: xv-3.0 is shareware!)
		- both have hacked TIFF libraries which work on Alpha
		  (robm@snail.ncsa.uiuc.edu (Rob McCool))
		  

Worst news last: There is software nobody in netland has managed to
compile on the Alpha. At least he/she didn't tell us. Help us to clear
this list!
	- X11R5
	- INN1.4
	- IDA sendmail
	- C-news

################################################################################
 
Part Two:	Known problems with hard- and software.

################################################################################
 
# How to distiguish OSF/1 BL10 from BL12
	- the file /usr/include/stamp.h defines INCLUDE_STAMP as "BL10",
	  whereas BL12 defines it as "3.11"
	- /etc/motd has something like 
	       "DEC OSF/1 T1.2-2  (Rev. 5)" for BL10 
	  and  "DEC OSF/1 V1.2 (Rev. 10)" for BL12
	  
# The scheduler is not running correctly. 
	You can watch it nice(!)ly using 'syd'.
	
	Kalle@rrz.uni-koeln.de (Claus Kalle) wrote in the mailing list:
	"It appears that nice values (talking about p_nice or p_basepri)
	do not have any meaning in adjusting CPU usage on or systems,
	too. At first I thought this to be a deficiency of BL10, but 
	the "feature" continued on BL12.
	
	DEC, is this a new way to adjust OSF/1 scheduling to Windows/NT ?"

	Jack Burness <Jack_Burness@kpc.com> wrote in the mailing list
	on 7 May 1993: "Digital is aware of this problem. They have it 
	corrected and it will be available in the maintenance release,
	which I believe is due out in the summer. We had a chance to 
	test a few of prototype fixes and they seem to work.
	 Note that on BL12 renice and nice commands themselves do work 
	(i.e. they correctly change the state in the system tables and 
	'ps' indicates that the jobs are running at the specified priority), 
	but the scheduler has a bug in it and so the priority information 
	really results in no changes to performance."
	
# man 	- If you want "less" as your pager use setenv PAGER 'less -sr'

# mount - If you have problems mounting a CD-ROM getting 
	  "/dev/rz4c on /cdrom: Dirty file system":
	  mount -d  will do it. It's missing in the (BL10-) manpage. I 
	  found it in an example section elsewhere. (Who can tell me?)

# pixie	- To get pixie to work on BL10, you:
	  		  %cc -O0 -non_shared <yourcode>
	  and then: 	  % pixie a.out
	  and then:	  % a.out.pixie
	  and then:	  % prof -pixie a.out a.out.Addrs  a.out.Counts
	
  	Thanks to Paul Howell <grue@engin.umich.edu> and Dave Haley at the 
  	Atlanta CSC for this info.
		  
# printer - Anybody with a postscript printer connected to a serial port ? 
	  
	 bruhn@aie.wu-wien.ac.at (Peter Bruhn) describes the problem:
	  "I have connected a LaserJet III (Postscript) to the serial 
	  interface of my DEC 3000/400 (/dev/tty00).  As long as I print 
	  short PS-files, everything is all right. But if I try to print 
	  a long one (1 MB), the printer stops after ca. 10-15 pages. 
	  "/etc/printcap" is not the problem, since I have tried to cat 
	  the file to /dev/tty00 directly, and this produced the same 
	  result."
	  
	  After some experiments with terminals connected to /dev/tty00 we
	  explained that behavior by assuming, that the port ignores flow 
	  control chars (^S/^Q) the connected device sends.  The printer 
	  is flooded with data and stops reading from /dev/tty00 when it's 
	  memory is exhausted.
	   Anybody to confirm or disagree? Anybody with a workaround?
	  If it works for you: Tell us, please, and send the output of 
	  "stty -a < /dev/tty00".
	   
# rshd 	- don't leave last position of /etc/passwd blank!
	  Although a null field in the last position of /etc/passwd 
	  implies /bin/sh as far as login(1) is concerned, the same 
	  isn't true for rshd.  You must explicitly specify /bin/sh 
	  if you want the Bourne shell.
	  error message: "rshd, : No such file or directory"
	  reported by Mark Bartelt <sysmark@chipmunk.cita.utoronto.ca>

