Having already bought a couple of AXP systems (a 3000/400S and a 3000/500S),
it seemed worth taking a look at the 3000/300.  We had a demo system in for
a few days, and one of the first things I did was to run our standard suite
of computationally-intensive benchmarks, to compare its performance with the
two AXP boxes that we already have.

There are a number of things I don't yet understand about the timings:  Most
are in the same ballpark as the 500S, which one might expect, given the two
systems' clock speeds.  However, one program (an FFT program, approximately
10Mb in size) requires between 1.4 (compiled with -O0) and 2.0 (compiled -O2)
the CPU time as it does on the 500S.  Any guesses as to why?

More worrying, though, are some nonreproducibilities.  The programs were run
on an otherwise idle 3000/300 (except for whatever daemons were started up
by DEC's boot-time stuff).  Each program was run three times, compiled at
each of five different -O levels.  For the most part, the user-mode CPU time
varied little with each set of three runs.  But there were a few hiccups:

	solar   -O1     16:55.0 19:58.1 17:03.7
and
	nbody   -O1     12:48.2 13:55.2 12:55.4
	nbody   -O2      7:35.0  7:36.1  9:03.0
and
	dsqrt   -O0      5:10.5  5:11.0 10:06.3   (!!)

Note that these are user-mode CPU times, not elapsed times (though, since
nothing else of consequence was being run, all the elapsed times were only
an augenblick longer than CPU times anyway).

Any insight as to what the heck is going on here?  We haven't noticed this
on our 400S or 500S (though I'll rerun our benchmark suite a few times to
be certain!).  The ~20% variance in the first two programs above would be
a cause for concern in itself, but the factor of nearly two in the "dsqrt"
program is particularly worrisome.

By the way, none of the oddities can be attributed to compiler differences,
since I just copied all the a.outs from the 500S to the 300.  The Fortran
programs were compiled with "-non_shared" on the 500S, since the demo system
didn't have Fortran on it, and therefore lacked the Fortran shared libraries.

One other possibly-relevant data point is the fact that the three programs
which showed the most variation in CPU times are all small (well, small by
today's standards -- between 500Kb and 1Mb).  The larger programs showed
very little run-to-run variation.

