In a previous article I described some oddities with nonreproducibility of CPU
times on a 3000/300 we had for evaluation.  There was another peculiarity with
that system, which sounds like it could be a kernel bug or a hardware problem.

In the absence of NFS cross-mounts, I generally transfer large hierarchies from
one system to another with some variant of

    rsh remotesys 'cd /src/dir; tar cBf - .' | ( cd /dest/dir; tar xBf - )
or
    ( cd /src/dir; tar cBf - . ) | rsh remotesys 'cd /dest/dir; tar xBf -'

I can do this between our 400S and our 500S, in either direction, initiating the
transfer from either system.  Moreover, if I try to move things from the 500S to
the 300, things work fine if I initiate the transfer from the 300 (by using the
first of the two methods above, with "remotesys" == the 500S).  However, whenever
I try to shove things from the 500S to the 300, by using the second method on the
500S (with "remotesys" == the 300), the transfer goes excruciatingly slowly (what
should take a few minutes takes an hour or more), and eventually everything stops.
Running "ps" on the two systems shows that tar is still active on both, but nothing
is getting transferred.  Furthermore, even killing off the "tar" processes on both
systems leaves things in an odd state, in which any attempt to run a command on the
300 by using "rsh" on the 500S will hang, until the "rshd" which is tar's parent on
the 300 is killed manually.

The 300, of course, doesn't have a "real" ethernet interface, just the 10BaseT
jack.  DEC supplied an external 10BaseT-to-thinwire box.  Any educated guesses
as to whether the problem is with this box, or with the 10BaseT hardware in the
300, or in the kernel's 10BaseT driver, or in the kernel's streams and/or socket
code, or ...?

I'd be curious to learn whether this is a known problem which will be fixed soon,
or whether there's something peculiar to our particular configuration (e.g. broken
hardware) that's more likely to account for it.  TIA.

