The most common reason for seeing approximately 20% performance
differences from run to run of a benchmark program is that the various
runs have different pages on the freelist, so end up with different
virtual->physical mappings. Some runs then by fluke get page mappings
that thrash in the direct-mapped cache. This effect is most pronounced
in benchmarks (Murphy rules :-)). There are a number of techniques to
minimize it, including the obvious set-associate caches, and OS memory
allocation policies that give the effect of bigger pages or at least the
identity mapping in several bits just above the offset-within-page
address bits, to the less obvious (to me) deliberate remapping of pages
to reduce thrashing.

I expect you will see some or all of these techniques used throughout
the industry over the next several years.
--
