In article <1sovun$lhc@agate.berkeley.edu> forrest@nobozo.CS.Berkeley.EDU (Jon Forrest) writes:
>So let's say you're a university site that is interested in adding
>value to OSF/1 the same way you added value to Unix. You've decided
>to accept DEC's word that OSF/1 is the Future and you're raring to
>go. So, you call OSF to find out what is required for an OSF/1 source
>license. The first thing is an AT&T Unix source license. Fair enough,
>you say, although you mutter something about what "Open Software"
>should really mean.

Please blame this one on AT&T (actually, USL), not DEC!  I'm sure
we'd be much happier if USL decided to stop charging for UNIX
licenses :-).

>Then, you find out that the OSF/1 source license
>itself costs $5,000 which, although not cheap, isn't excessive. So
>far, so good. Then, and here's the kicker, you find out that if
>you buy a license for OSF/1 now you're getting a license for OSF/1
>version 1.2, and when version 1.3 comes out, YOU HAVE TO PAY $5,000
>again. When version 1.4 comes out, you have to pay $5,000 again.
>Likewise for versions 1.5, 1.6, and so on.

Again, this is OSF, Inc.'s policy, not DEC's.

>If I were getting a complete set of sources and documentation with
>each release then I could see how it would be reasonable to have to
>pay for each release. But, since we want nothing from OSF except
>the license since we're going to be getting our sources from DEC,
>having to pay $5,000 for each release doesn't seem right. But,
>OSF doesn't just implement this policy for OSF/1 - this is the policy
>for all their software products!!

I'm not sure you actually will need to buy a new OSF Inc. license
for each OSF release that you get from DEC.  Another way of putting
that: I don't think our release numbering has much to do with OSF's.
In other words, DEC OSF/1 V1.3 probably will come under the same OSF
source license as DEC OSF/1 V1.2.  But I could be wrong!

I guess the only way to be sure will be to order the DEC OSF/1 V1.3
sources, when they become available, and see if DEC makes you get
a new OSF/1 source license.  If we do, then please complain!

By the way, DEC's OSF/1 source license *for educational institutions*
is part number QB-MT4BA-E8 and lists for $1180 (in the US).  I believe
you *do* have to pay for a new one of these for each release of DEC
OSF/1.

Also, the OSF Inc. $5000 license is a "site license", so you only
need one for your entire University (which might not be a big help
in some places, but it might be a win for UC Berkley.)

I'm interested in this issue because I am sponsoring DEC external
research grants for a couple of university researchers who would
like to modify OSF/1, and DEC grant money cannot be used to pay
for the OSF, Inc. license.  That means that I have to convince
my grant recipients to spend $5000 of their own money on this.

I strongly encourage people at educational sites, if you think
you might one day want to work on OSF/1 sources, to contact OSF
Inc. and suggest that they modify their source licensing policies
if you think they are inappropriate.

-Jeff


