We have a DEC 5000 /300 running OSF 1.2 and have a problem with nfs mounts.
This system has been added to the /etc/exports files of several other
systems, but when we try to mount the remote filesystems,
a message such as the following occurs:

# mount -a -t nfs
Can't access /patients/p3: Permission denied

Our /etc/fstab has an entry like this:

/patients/p3@axon       /patients/p3    nfs rw,bg 0 0

and the exports file on the remote system (axon) has an entry like this:

/patients/p3    -root=axon:brain:monet,access=uofcmri:mrisun:brain:monet:axon:cranio:fusion:alpha:pissarro

The remote system in this example is a Sun, but the same problem occurs when
trying to mount file systems on a Decstation 5000 running Ultrix 4.2a.  

NFS was set up on the Alpha using the "nfssetup" script and taking all
the defaults.  Remote machines have no trouble mounting alpha's filesystems;
it is only when alpha tries to mount theirs that problems occur.

Is this a common problem, or is there something obvious that I've missed?

